WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:09.094 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Asia episode six of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.746 --> 00:00:27.206 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Six map targets form our East Asian route: China, Mongolia, Taiwan, North
Korea, South Korea and Japan. Taiwan’s separate target is a geographic
teaching choice, not a settlement of its political status.

3
00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:30.678 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring China and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:35.163 --> 00:00:48.213 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
China is the broad eastern mainland country south of Mongolia and Russia. The
Korean Peninsula and Japan lie beyond its eastern side; India and the
Himalayan countries are to the southwest.

5
00:00:48.865 --> 00:01:01.613 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Great Wall was developed across many periods, joining and extending older
defences. It is a network with a long history, rather than one wall built in a
single project.

6
00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:13.235 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a long defensive frontier across northern China, below Mongolia. Use
the broad mainland outline first, then place the smaller eastern neighbours
around it.

7
00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.786 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Mongolia and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:32.623 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mongolia is the large landlocked country between Russia and China. It has no
direct border with Kazakhstan, despite coming close to its eastern end.

9
00:01:33.275 --> 00:01:46.278 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Orkhon Valley contains the remains of Karakorum, a capital of the Mongol
Empire. Pastureland and traces of imperial government sit together in the same
cultural landscape.

10
00:01:46.941 --> 00:01:58.621 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember a steppe country sandwiched between two giants, Russia above and
China below. Picture an imperial capital amid grazing lands, rather than
looking for a coast.

11
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.599 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Taiwan and its neighbours into view.

12
00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:16.989 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Taiwan is the island off China’s southeastern coast, north of the Philippines
and southwest of Japan’s main islands. The Taiwan Strait separates it from the
mainland.

13
00:02:17.653 --> 00:02:31.341 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Taipei’s National Palace Museum holds a jadeite cabbage with tiny carved
insects. The artist used the stone’s natural green and white colours to
imitate leaves and stalk.

14
00:02:31.998 --> 00:02:43.596 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture the jade cabbage on the island across the strait. Taiwan is
self-governed and claimed by China; we follow its separate GeoClick target
without treating the map as a settlement of political status.

15
00:02:44.276 --> 00:02:47.817 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring North Korea and its neighbours into view.

16
00:02:50.421 --> 00:03:00.231 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Korea occupies the northern part of the Korean Peninsula, below China
and above South Korea. A short northeastern border touches Russia.

17
00:03:00.892 --> 00:03:11.643 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ancient Koguryo tombs preserve painted scenes of daily life and beliefs. Their
history reaches back many centuries before the modern division of Korea.

18
00:03:12.296 --> 00:03:23.047 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember painted tombs in the northern half of the peninsula. The two Korean
map targets are neighbours on the same peninsula; Japan is across the sea to
the east.

19
00:03:25.376 --> 00:03:36.765 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
South Korea occupies the southern part of the Korean Peninsula, below North
Korea. Its shores face seas on three sides, with Japan across the water to the
east.

20
00:03:37.422 --> 00:03:49.020 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Haeinsa Temple safeguards Buddhist texts carved on around eighty thousand
wooden printing blocks. Their purpose-built storage buildings helped preserve
this enormous wooden library.

21
00:03:49.673 --> 00:04:02.676 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture a library made of carved wood in the peninsula’s southern half. North
Korea above, South Korea below: the paired location comes before the extra
fact.

22
00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.656 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Japan and its neighbours into view.

23
00:04:15.677 --> 00:04:27.914 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Japan is the island arc east of Korea and the Asian mainland. Its main islands
run roughly northeast to southwest, with Hokkaido toward the north and Kyushu
toward the south.

24
00:04:28.581 --> 00:04:43.639 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yakushima, south of Kyushu, preserves ancient Japanese cedar trees. This
forested island is a reminder that Japan includes exceptional natural
landscapes as well as densely built cities.

25
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:54.958 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture old cedar trees on the island arc beyond Korea. Keep Japan offshore,
and remember that the main islands are only part of a much wider island
country.

26
00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:19.733 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
China is the broad mainland, Mongolia the landlocked country above it. Taiwan
lies off the southeastern coast; North and South Korea share a peninsula, and
Japan forms the island arc beyond them.

27
00:05:20.392 --> 00:05:37.276 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Recall the long-built wall, steppe capital, jade cabbage, painted tombs,
wooden printing library and ancient cedar forest. Asian Russia continues north
of this group; we orient it here without repeating the Europe lesson.
